+# hvir
+
+**hvir** is a lightweight, view-first workbench for agentic development, built with Electron, React, and TypeScript. It combines terminal sessions, file viewing, Git workflows, document review, session tracking, and web panes in a single desktop application.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Node.js **24+**
+- npm
+- macOS or Linux for packaging/smoke workflows in this repository
+
+## Getting started
+
+```bash
+npm ci
+npm run dev
+```
+
+The app uses `electron-vite` for local development and builds native runtime dependencies during install.
+
+## Common commands
+
+| Command | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `npm run dev` | Start the Electron app in development mode |
+| `npm run build` | Type-check and create a production build |
+| `npm run test` | Run the Vitest suite |
+| `npm run lint` | Run ESLint |
+| `npm run typecheck` | Run TypeScript checks for node/web targets |
+| `npm run verify` | Full policy/lint/type/test verification gate |
+| `npm run smoke` | Run production Electron smoke scenarios |
+| `npm run smoke:scenario -- <scenario...>` | Run selected smoke scenario(s) |
+
+## Architecture at a glance
+
+- `src/main` — Electron main-process runtime, IPC handler wiring, project/workspace orchestration, PTY/Git/document-review coordination.
+- `src/preload` — secure bridge exposing the typed `window.hvir` API and enforcing IPC contract boundaries.
+- `src/renderer` — React UI for workspaces, terminals, file viewer/diff, Git panels/graph, sessions, document review, settings, and web panes.
+- `src/workers` — utility-process workers (including Git worker isolation paths).
+- `src/shared` — shared contracts and types (IPC, workspace, diagnostics, Git, viewer, sessions, terminal behaviors).
+- `test` — Vitest unit/integration coverage and smoke/acceptance policy fixtures.
+
+## Packaging and platform-specific workflows
+
+Examples of repository-provided packaging commands:
+
+- `npm run pack:mac:arm64`
+- `npm run pack:linux:x64`
+- `npm run pack:linux:arm64`
+
+Additional SSH acceptance and installed-package smoke commands are available under `scripts/` and exposed via `package.json` scripts.
